# Frosthollow (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): mining and snow, a high valley in the Whitefangs. Short, dry, dark
# humour; everything is rock, seam, lamp and cold. Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar frosthollow
greet = Warm hearth | Hearth to you
greet.broad = 'Arth | 'Arth and lamp
farewell = Keep your lamp lit | Lamp lit
agree = Sound as rock | Aye, sound
refuse = Dead seam, that | Not a chance
surprise = Well, strike me | Cold iron
annoyed = Slag and ashes | Cave it all
very = deep
good = sound | solid
bad = cracked | dead
worthless = dead rock | slag
luck = a good seam
child = whelp
pet!! = old stone | lamp of mine
money = brass
cold = cold | biting | iron-cold
tired = spent | done in
nonsense = slag | fool's gold
tag = , eh
tag.broad = , eh, eh
oath! = Black seam | Cold iron | Lamp and pick
home = my own hearth
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = stone | cousin
address[other.stage=young] = whelp | young 'un
bed = my bunk | the straw
dear! = my lamp | my stone | hearth-heart
drunk = lamp-lit | well oiled
hot = close as a forge | stifling
hungry = hollow | empty as a worked seam
hurt = cracked | bruised
little = a chip | a splinter
rain = sleet | wet
robbery = That's claim-jumping, that is | Picking my seam, they are
saying! = Rock's rock | Dig, and don't look up | The seam goes where it goes
thanks = Lamp's thanks | Thanks, stone
work = the seam | the shift | graft
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = brass | the shift's brass
boss = the gaffer | the foreman | the old stone
busy = run off my feet | deep in it
slow = dead as a worked seam | quiet
takings = the day's brass
cheated = short-weighed | sold fool's gold
mum! = Mam | Mother
dad! = Da | Father
gran = Nan | Old Mam
grandad = Grandda | Old Da
supper = supper | the pot
toffs = the city lamps | the soft-pawed lot
skint = out of brass | dug out
posh = soft-pawed | polished
busybody = pit-gossip | rattler
fool = you lump of slag | you daft stone
daft = cracked | seam-struck
toast = Lamp and luck | Deep seams | Warm hearths
drink = a jar | a pot of dark
snow = snow | the white | drift
wind = the Whitefang wind | a blow
ale = dark ale | pit-ale
watch = the Watch | the lamp-men
thief = claim-jumper | thief
bandits = pass-robbers | road wolves
grave = the cold ground | under the stone
grub = snap | grub
snug = snug as a lamp-room | warm
poorly = chesty | poorly
chill = the pit-cough | a chill
remedy = goose grease | a hot stone in flannel | pine-tar
olddays = the deep years | the old seams
blessing = Lamp keep you | Keep your lamp lit | Warm hearth to you
bless = Lamp keep you | Hearth keep you
far = a long climb | down the valley and over
stranger = valley-stranger | lowlander
# The dialogue window (doc 83).
shop_offer = Come in from the cold and look. | What'll warm you? | Look your fill, cousin.
shop_dear = Winter's in the prices. | Dear as firewood in a frost. | Nothing's cheap up here.
shop_sold = Wrap it well. | There, against the cold. | Yours, cousin.
shop_bye = Keep your lamp lit. | Lamp lit, cousin. | Mind the ice.
shop_bare = Bare as a winter field. | The snow took the rest. | Little left till the thaw.
talk_open = What brings you to my hearth? | Speak, cousin. | Something the matter?
# A giver's work (doc 83, Phase 2).
work_offer = There's work, cousin, if you've the legs for it. | I could use a steady pair of paws. | Something wants doing before the snow.
work_taken = Good. Don't let it freeze. | Good. Keep your lamp lit on the road. | Settled, then.
work_paid = Here, as promised, cousin. | Paid, and a warm hearth to you. | Fairly done. There you are.
work_back = No harm done, cousin. Another will take it. | Fair enough. Better said by the fire. | Better now than in the snow.
work_none = Nothing going, cousin. | No work this side of the thaw. | Nothing that needs doing.
# The other roles (doc 83, Phase 3).
inn_offer = Come in to the hearth, cousin. A bed or a bowl? | Warm yourself. What'll it be? | Bed, bowl or a cup?
inn_bed = Upstairs, by the chimney. There's a chest for your things. | Your bed's warm upstairs. Keep your lamp lit.
watch_greet = The watch, stone. Speak plain. | Keep the peace, cousin, and we'll get on.
church_greet = Lamp keep you. Come in. | Warm hearth, and welcome. All are welcome here.
learn_offer = It's a trade that keeps the hearth lit. | Hard work, but it's honest.
# The dialogue trees' other words (doc 83, Phase 4): news and gossip there isn't, a trouble that isn't, thanks for
# help, the way, a resident's work, work turned down, an inn's fire and its helpers, the watch's word, a master's
# new apprentice, something to hand over; a keeper asleep.
shop_asleep = Mm... fire's banked... come back at first light. | Zzz... shift's over, cousin. Morning.
news_none = Quiet as a banked fire. | Nothing new down the valley. | Not a word, cousin.
gossip_none = Pit-gossip's not for me. | Nothing I'd repeat, cousin.
trouble_none = Nothing a hearth won't mend. | Nothing, cousin.
trouble_thanks = Warm hearth to you for that. I won't forget it. | You're sound, cousin. Thank you.
way_ask = Where to, cousin? | Lost in the valley?
job_busy = This and that, cousin. | I keep at it.
work_declined = Suit yourself, cousin. | Another shift, then.
inn_rest = Sit close to the hearth, cousin. A bed upstairs rests you properly. | Take the bench by the fire. Thaw out.
watch_noted = Noted, cousin. We'll dig them out. | Good. We'll find them.
watch_bye = Keep your lamp lit and the peace kept. | Move along, cousin.
learn_taken = Good. Be here at the shift's start, and stay near me. | Then you're mine to teach, cousin. Stay close.
receiver_ask = Something for me, cousin? | For me? Come in from the cold.
inn_helper = I only help here, cousin. Ask #here.innkeeper# for a bed. | #here.innkeeper# keeps the hearth and the rooms. I carry the cups.

# Discourse words (doc 84 §10.3, Phase 7): Frosthollow's hedge, account, preclose, excuse and the rest (plain.grammar
# says what each is). Drafts, to be tuned by ear.
hedge = Look | Hold up, stone | Well
account = My hands are full as a coal sack | I've my own seam to dig
ack = Right, then, stone | So that's the seam
pivot = Same seam, that. | Which reminds me, stone.
anyway = Anyway. That seam's worked out. | Enough of that rock.
later = next shift | after the shift's out
resume = Back to the seam | As I was saying, stone
preclose = Well. The lamp's burning down | Still. Rock won't dig itself
excuse = The hearth's calling | The shift won't wait
knew = Heard it down the pit | Old seam, that news
sympathy = That's a cold seam | Heavy rock to carry
concede = I'll give you that seam | You may be right, stone
insist = I'll not shift on it | Mark me, stone
warn = Mind the roof | Keep your lamp high
welcome_back = Back up from the pit | Back again, stone
welcome = Pull up a rock | Room by the lamp
sorry = No offence, stone | I spoke out of turn
forgive = No harm done, stone | Forgotten, like a worked seam
deflect = No bother, stone | Don't mention it, stone
no_matter = Another shift, then | No matter, stone
go_ahead = Dig in, stone | Out with it, stone
put_off = Not this shift | After I've come up
vent = Between you and me and the rock | Truth be told, stone
reassure = You'll dig through it | It'll hold, stone
congrats = A good seam, that | Struck rich, you have
demur = That seam won't pay | I'd not dig there
doubt = Fool's gold, maybe | Show me the seam first
listen = Listen, stone | Here

# Backchannel lines (doc 84 §10.2, Phase 7): plain's short answers ("Good.", "Always.", "It is.") as Frosthollow says them.
# Drafts, to be tuned by ear; one left out falls back to plain's words.
bc_good = Sound. | Solid.
bc_always = Like the cold. | Since the first seam.
bc_thank_you = Lamp's thanks. | Thanks, stone.
bc_it_is = It is, sound as rock.
bc_ha = Heh. Dark, that. | Ha. Cold, but funny.
bc_i_will = I will, sure as rock.
bc_very = Deep. | Seam-deep.
bc_never = Never, not down here. | Never, not once.
bc_probably = Likely. | Like as not, down here.
bc_we_will_see = We'll see where the seam goes. | We'll see, when the dust settles.
bc_maybe = Might. | Maybe. Maybe it's fool's gold.
bc_me_too = Same seam. | Same here, stone.
bc_it_does = It does. Sound. | Aye, it does.
bc_again = Again. Same old seam. | Again, cave it.
bc_lovely = Bright as a fresh seam. | Lovely as lamplight.
bc_every_year = Every year, like the first snow. | Every winter, same.
bc_wise = Sound thinking. | Wise, that. Rock-wise.
bc_i_know = Know it like my own seam. | I know it, stone.
bc_so_they_say = So they say, down the pit. | So the lamp-room says.
bc_again_q = Again? Cave it. | What, again?
bc_of_course = Course. | Of course, stone.
bc_about_time = About time. Slag-slow. | High time.
bc_mine_too = Same seam. | Mine too, stone.
bc_ill_try = I'll have a dig at it. | I'll try. Sound.
bc_nor_me = Not me neither, stone. | Nor me, stone.
bc_they_do = Aye, they do. | They do. Sound.
bc_poor_soul = Poor stone. | Poor soul. Cold luck.
bc_i_wont = I won't. Rock-sure. | Won't.
bc_every_one = Every last chip. | Every one, stone.
bc_not_me = Not this stone.
bc_very_good = Solid as rock. | Sound, that.
bc_go_on_then = Go on, then. Dig. | Go on, stone.
bc_barely = Barely scraping. | By a splinter.
bc_lucky_you = Good seam, that. | Lucky old stone.
bc_every_night = Every night, end of shift. | Every night, lamp to lamp.
bc_nobody = Nobody. Empty seam. | Not a lamp in sight.
bc_for_once = For once. Chalk it up.
bc_i_hope_so = Here's hoping, stone. | Hope the seam holds.
bc_exactly = Struck true. | Spot on the seam.
bc_everything = The whole seam. | Lamp, pick and all.
bc_true = True as granite. | Sound.
bc_mostly = Mostly, give or take a seam.
bc_gladly = Gladly, stone. | Gladly. Sound.
bc_fair_enough = Fair enough, stone. | Sound enough.
bc_sorry = Sorry, stone.
bc_every_time = Every shift, regular as the pick.
bc_very_bad = Bad as slag. | Cracked, that.
bc_fair = Square, that. | Fair. Sound.
bc_bless_you = Bless you, stone. | Lamp bless you.
bc_always_is = Always is, down here. | Always was, always is.
bc_right = Right, stone.
bc_lucky = A good seam. | Lucky, that.
bc_bad_luck = Cracked luck. | Bad seam, that.
bc_why_q = Why's that, stone?
bc_true_enough = Sound enough. | True enough, stone.
bc_they_always_do = Always do. | They always do, down here.
bc_every_day = Every shift, down the pit. | Every day, lamp to lamp.
bc_very_busy = Run off my feet. | Deep in it.
bc_im_sorry = I'm sorry, stone.
bc_so_do_i = So do I, stone. | Same seam.
bc_clever = Sharp as a pick. | Clever, like a well-timed charge.
bc_too_late = Too late. Caved in. | Too late, stone.
bc_go_on = Go on. Dig. | Go on, stone.
bc_we_will = We will. Sound. | That we will.
bc_they_are = That they are, stone. | They are. Sound.
bc_so_am_i = So am I, stone. | Same seam.
bc_let_them = Let them dig. | Let them, stone.
bc_hope_so = Here's hoping, stone. | Hope the seam holds.
bc_already = Dug and done. | Already, stone.
bc_i_might = Might do. | I might, stone.
bc_the_best = Best seam going. | The best, solid.
bc_somebody_has_to = Somebody has to dig it. | Somebody has to, stone.
bc_nothing = Nothing. Bare rock. | Not a chip.
bc_and_q = And then, stone?
bc_who_q = Who's that, stone?
bc_it_was = It was. Sound. | That it was, stone.
